  "notes": "⚠ **`confidence` is low, and the research says why: the lane is clear and the technical sourcing is thin — the whole entry is marked [LOW-CONF].** What is genuinely sourced is the lane itself: drawled cadence-driven Miami trap, riff-over-808 productions with little or no chordal content, chanted hooks in a low register, short tracks. Everything numeric below is INFERRED from those four prose lines — `kick.syncopation` 0.5, `hihat.fillDensity` 0.5, the whole `bass808` block and every melody figure. Treat it as a sketch of the lane rather than a model of the records, and it is the first entry on this axis to redo when a real production account appears. ⛔ It is authored on the fast Miami feel (95–105 straight, clap on 2 and 4) rather than the 130–145 half-time alternative the research also names, because that is the feel the cited record is built on. ⛔ `chords.impliedByRiff` is set — \"little or no chordal content\" is as close to chord-less as the research gets — and the chord part's variety is carried by `chordDurationBeats` [4,16] plus three `harmonicRhythm` values plus a wide voicing register, not by invented families. ⚠ `melody.register` [64,84] is the lowest on this whole axis and it is the one INFERRED number with a direct textual basis: \"hooks chanted in a low register\". ⚠ `anticipationProb` raised to 0.15. With `mirror_kick` against a fully locked 808 both parts take every kick, so every bass onset lands exactly on an 808 onset — `coherence.rs` calls that one line played twice rather than two parts locking. The lock is the style and is untouched; the anticipation is what makes them two parts.",
